## Persistent Data Directory

`/data` is a persistent directory mounted from the host. Use it to cache downloaded
datasets, models, and other large files so they survive across runs:

```bash
# Example: download a dataset only if not already cached
if [ ! -d /data/my_dataset ]; then
    huggingface-cli download my-org/my-dataset --local-dir /data/my_dataset
fi
ln -s /data/my_dataset ./my_dataset  # symlink into your working dir
```

Always check `/data` before downloading anything large. Store HuggingFace datasets,
git-cloned repos, and pip wheels there when possible.

During literature review, actively search for existing implementations on GitHub and
Papers With Code. Clone relevant repos into `experiment_codebase/cloned_repos/` to study
and build on — don't write code from scratch when a reference implementation exists.
Start by creating `experiment_codebase/README.md` to document your experiment setup.

## Git Repository (Version History)

Your workspace (`/app`) is a git repo connected to GitLab. An initial commit has
already been made on your branch. **Commit and push after each major milestone** so
your work is saved remotely and has full version history:

```bash
cd /app && git add -A && git commit -m "describe what changed" && git push
```

Good commit points: after literature review, after experiments run, after paper draft,
after reviewer feedback addressed.

### Learn from Previous Runs

Other branches in this repo are previous runs of the **same idea** — each contains a
full workspace (experiment code, results, paper drafts, figures, and reviewer feedback).
Checking what was already tried can save significant time and avoid repeating mistakes.

Previous runs (branches):
{{GITLAB_BRANCHES}}

Before diving in, consider inspecting the most recent branches. Reviewer feedback from
previous runs is especially valuable — it identifies what worked and what still needs
fixing. Example commands:

```bash
# List all branches
git branch -a

# Read the paper from a previous run
git show origin/<branch>:latex/template.tex | head -200

# Check experiment results
git show origin/<branch>:experiment_codebase/README.md

# Read reviewer feedback (most useful — tells you what to fix)
git show origin/<branch>:submissions/v1_*/reviewer_communications/response.md

# List what files exist on another branch
git ls-tree --name-only -r origin/<branch> | head -50
```

Use your judgment on what to inspect — if there are many previous branches, focus on
the most recent ones and their reviewer feedback.

## Artifact Collection

Save artifacts incrementally throughout the run — don't wait until the end. After each major milestone (experiments done, plots done, paper compiled, review received), copy what you have so far:

```bash
mkdir -p /logs/agent/artifacts
cp -r experiment_codebase/ /logs/agent/artifacts/
cp -r figures/ /logs/agent/artifacts/
cp -r literature/ /logs/agent/artifacts/literature/ 2>/dev/null
cp latex/template.pdf /logs/agent/artifacts/paper.pdf
cp latex/template.tex /logs/agent/artifacts/paper.tex
cp latex/references.bib /logs/agent/artifacts/references.bib 2>/dev/null
cp -r submissions/ /logs/agent/artifacts/submissions/ 2>/dev/null
```

This ensures partial work is preserved if the run times out.

> **Warning:** Only `/logs/agent/` and `/logs/verifier/` are mounted to the host. Do NOT write to `/logs/artifacts/` or any other `/logs/` subpath — they are ephemeral and will be lost.

Follow the research process defined in your project instructions.
